    title = {Journal, 1790-1791},
    year = {1790},
    url = {http://www.uaf.edu/anla/item.xml?id=ALE791M1978},
    anlanote = {(2) First printed edition (partial) in German, contains ca. 194 Aleut items. (3) Russian translation, contains 214 Aleut items, including months, and alphabetic list, pp. 93-97. (4) includes 21 Aleut bird-names, directly from (1), with indentifications, pp. 120-124; information republished in Theed Pearse, Birds of the Early Explorers in the Northern Pacific, Comox, B.C., 1968, pp. 75-87. (5) English translation, includes ca. 260 items, esp. pp. 66-91, 162-179.The Aleut terms in these editions by no means completely overlap, the original ms. perhaps containing over 300 items, especially flora-fauna and cultural terms not on the standard Backmeister-Pallas-Robek list; the published spellings of the same items often differ widely. Many of the terms were incorporated by Merck from the journal (not located) of his assistant Carl Krebs (Stresemann 1950.110, Pearse 1968.77, 81, 86-87; see also Pierce 1980.32; according to Stresemann, the marginal notes in (1) are from Kreb's journal). L. Black (Eskimo Motifs in Aleut Art and Folklore, Etudes/Inuit/Studies 7(1):3-23 (1983), p. 12) notes, "Merck obtained his information with the aid of…Robeck (who engaged in linguistic investigations, collecting standard vocabulary according to a chart designed by Pallas) and through an Aleut interpreter, who spoke Russian, Mikhail Lavrent'evich Milkov of Umnak." Merck (Pierce 1980.141) transmitting standard lists to Pallas from Robck, credits Robek with "teaching" him all the native words underlined in his journal},
    anlclanguage = {Aleut (Unangan)},
    anlctype = {lexicon},
    comments = {Formerly ALE790M1790. Also in collection: bibliographic entry from original folder and notes from inside cover, 1 p.; handwritten notes of bibliographic additions to folder; Merck, Carl Heinrich. Ethnographic Observations Concerning the Peoples of the Bering Sea (1789-91). Translated by Richard A. Pierce. Translation Project of Material Relating to Alaska Natives. Prepared for Alaska Division of State Libraries, 1974. 8 pp.; Table of contents in Russian, 1 p., seems to have something to do with Billings expedition.; Pearse, Theed. "Carl Heinrich Merck: The Billings Expedition," from Birds of the Early Explorers in the Northern Pacific. Comox, BC: Theed Pearse, 1968. 16 pp.; 2 pp., mention of Merck, unknown source; biography of Merck from unknown source, 3 pp. (2) became ALE791M1937, no longer in folder},
    format = {Text},
    glottolog_ref_id = {173138},
    lgcode = {Aleut [ale]}
}
