    citation = {WAIL/SSILA July 2001},
    comments = {"Goals:Examine the distribution of y- and b- as oblique objects comparatively within the Athapaskan family to determine their semantics, with a focus on b-. While the distribution of y- and b- is usually looked at just with third personsubjects (2i), we will look at the distribution of b- with first, second, and thirdperson subjects.Conclusion: While y- can be identified as a marker of focus, or obviative status, b- cannot be identified with topic, or proximate status; it is rather the default, or- elsewhere, case which occurs when a pronoun is required and y- is not available. (See Aissen 2000 for arguments for an obviation system [for Navajo] rather than a topic/focus system.)},
