    anlanote = {Ts. Book.Preface: The novel Elnuq in the Yup'ik Eskimo language of South-western Alaska in which it was composed, was published in 1990 by the Alaska Native Language Center at the University of Alaska Fairbanks. It had been written for the most part some fifteen years earlier under the auspices of the Center's Native Writers Project. It is still (in 2008) the only full-length work of creative writing published in Yup'ik.Some years after publication in Yup'ik it was translated into English by Steven Jacobson, and edited by the author. This is not a word for word translation, although it does follow the original Yup'ik fairly closely. Page numbers referring to the original Yup'ik version have been included in the English text, because it is expected that student of the Yup'ik language may find the translation helpful in reading the original...Though not autobiographical, the story of Eluguq draws on the memories of the author who spent her early life in the home of her ancestors, Iqsalleq, a small, remote, and now long-abandoned settlement of Yup'ik Eskimos in the mountains on a tributary of the Kuskokwim River in southwestern Alaska. Her life there was not untypical of Alaska Natives at that time, and this is the world portrayed in this novel.128 pp.Also in collection earlier draft: Elnguq, 1970 CY984J2008 f.1},
    anlclanguage = {Yup'ik-Central-Alaskan},
